        <div id="will-it-stick" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Will Paint Stick to Your Concrete Floor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It will if the surface is open, dry and rough enough, and three quick checks tell you which of those you have. Paint lets go of concrete for three reasons: something on the surface blocks the bond, moisture rising through the slab pushes the film off, or the surface is too smooth to grip. Each check takes an afternoon and tools you already own, and skipping them explains why so many painted floors peel inside a year.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is a sealer in the way? Try the water-drop test</h3>

          <p>
            Sprinkle a few tablespoons of water in several places, including the middle of the floor and near the walls. If the concrete darkens and absorbs the water within about a minute, the surface is open and ready. If the water beads or sits on top, a sealer, curing compound, wax or old coating is present. Paint would bond to that layer rather than to the concrete and come away with it, so the slab has to be stripped or mechanically ground before painting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is moisture rising through the slab? Try the plastic-sheet test</h3>

          <p>
            Tape an 18 by 18 inch square of clear plastic to the floor with all four edges sealed, and leave it for at least 16 hours, the period ASTM D4263 specifies; a full day is better. Droplets under the plastic, or concrete beneath it that looks darker than the surrounding slab, mean moisture is moving up through the floor, and paint will blister and peel. Solve the moisture first, using our guide to <a href="/how-to-fix-a-damp-basement/">fixing a damp basement</a> for a below-grade slab, or switch to a moisture-tolerant system.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is the surface rough enough? Try the touch and tape test</h3>

          <p>
            Run a palm across the floor. A paint-ready surface feels like 120 to 150 grit sandpaper. If it feels as smooth as glass, as power-troweled garage slabs often do, the paint has nothing to key into and needs an acid etch or a light grind first. On a previously painted floor, press duct tape firmly onto a few spots and rip it off; any paint that lifts with the tape must be removed before the new coat goes down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Prep Concrete Before Painting?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clear, degrease, profile, patch, vacuum and dry, in that order, and expect the prep to take longer than the painting. Every floor paint label starts by saying the surface must be clean, dry, sound and free of contaminants, and that one line carries most of the weight: paint performs only as well as the surface it is bonded to. Budget a full day of prep for a two-car garage and half a day for a basement room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you clean and degrease the floor?</h3>

          <p>
            Empty the room completely and scrape off anything stuck down, such as drywall mud, paint drips and adhesive. Sweep, then degrease. Oil and transmission fluid destroy adhesion, and a mop and bucket will not lift them. Scrub stained spots with a concrete degreaser and a stiff brush, rinse, and repeat until water no longer beads there. For old, deep stains, our guide to <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">getting oil out of concrete</a> covers poultices and stronger cleaners.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Should you acid etch or grind?</h3>

          <p>
            Most residential slabs need an acid etch: the citric acid etch sold in garage-floor kits, or diluted muriatic acid for harder, smoother concrete. Wet the floor, spread the etch evenly, scrub it in, and look for gentle fizzing, the sign that it is working. Rinse thoroughly, at least twice, and let the slab dry completely. Very smooth or very hard floors respond better to a rented diamond grinder, which also removes thin old coatings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When should cracks be filled?</h3>

          <p>
            After profiling and before painting. Use a concrete patch or crack filler that its manufacturer says can be painted, and give it time to cure fully. Then vacuum the entire floor with a shop vacuum, because dust left behind by etching or grinding is a hidden bond-breaker that no amount of good paint can overcome.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long should the slab dry before you paint?</h3>

          <p>
            After a wash or an etch, allow 24 to 48 hours, and longer in a cool, humid basement. Brand-new concrete is a separate case: give it at least 28 days of cure before any paint goes on, then run the water-drop and plastic-sheet checks as you would on an older slab.
          </p>

