        <div id="rolling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Paint a Concrete Floor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prime if the label asks for it, cut in the edges, then roll two thin coats on a mild, dry day and respect the recoat window. Start with the weather forecast rather than the paint aisle, because temperature and humidity decide how well the first coat bonds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What temperature should it be?</h3>

          <p>
            Most floor paints want air and slab temperatures roughly between 50 and 90 degrees Fahrenheit, with the slab at least 5 degrees above the dew point so condensation cannot form under the wet film. Painting a garage in morning sun, while the slab is still cold and the air is warming quickly, invites bubbles and a weak bond. Late morning on a mild, dry day is the best window.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do you need a primer?</h3>

          <p>
            Use the recommended primer whenever the label calls for one on bare concrete. Some epoxy-modified paints are self-priming when the first coat is thinned or applied lightly; others require a dedicated concrete primer, especially on porous or chalky slabs. Next, brush in the edges along walls and around posts a few feet at a time, so the cut line is still wet when the roller reaches it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which roller and technique work best?</h3>

          <p>
            Use a 3/8 to 1/2 inch nap roller on a pole, chosen to suit the label and the slab texture. Work in sections of about 4 by 6 feet, laying the paint on in a W pattern and then smoothing it with parallel strokes back into the wet edge. Keep each coat thin. A heavy first coat feels productive, but it traps solvent, stays soft longer and is more likely to peel. Two thin coats always beat one thick one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long between coats, and before you use the floor?</h3>

          <p>
            Follow the recoat window printed on the can. Recoat too soon and the second coat softens the first; wait too long on some epoxy-modified paints and the first coat cures enough that the second will not bond without scuffing. After the final coat, light foot traffic is usually fine in about a day, furniture in a few days and vehicles after roughly a week. Cool weather stretches every one of those times.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE CURE CLOCK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Dry to the touch does not mean cured.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A floor that feels dry after four hours is still soft underneath. Plasticizers in tires bond to under-cured paint and tear it away when the car backs out. Wait the full vehicle cure on the label, and longer when it is cool, before you park on it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Much Concrete Floor Paint Do You Need, and What Does It Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A two-car garage takes roughly 3 gallons for two coats, and the paint for it usually runs about $120 to $210 at the time of writing. The exact amount depends on the spread rate on your can and on how much the first coat soaks into the slab.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How far does a gallon go?</h3>

          <p>
            Smooth floor paints commonly list about 300 to 400 square feet per gallon per coat on smooth, sealed-pore concrete, and noticeably less on porous or freshly etched slabs, where the first coat soaks in. Textured coatings cover much less, often a quarter to a third of that area, because they go on thick. Always plan from the rate printed on the specific product rather than a generic figure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do the numbers work for common spaces?</h3>

          <p>
            A standard two-car garage measures around 400 to 500 square feet, so two coats of a smooth one-part paint mean about 800 to 1,000 square feet of coverage, or roughly 3 gallons plus a margin for a porous first coat. A one-car garage needs about half that. A 12 by 16 foot basement room, at 192 square feet, usually takes one gallon for two coats with a little left over for touch-ups.
          </p>

