          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does the whole job cost?</h3>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, mainstream one-part floor paints run roughly $40 to $70 a gallon, with pro-line products and premium brands toward the top of that range. Add roughly $40 to $80 for etch, degreaser, rollers, a brush, tape and a crack filler, plus $60 to $100 per day if you rent a floor grinder. Even with grinding, a painted two-car garage usually comes in well under the price of the cheapest two-part epoxy kit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why buy every can at once?</h3>

          <p>
            Buy all the paint in one trip, and if it is tinted, ask for a single batch or box the cans together before you start. Small tint differences between cans show up clearly on a floor seen from a low angle. Keep a quart of leftover paint, labeled with the room and the date, for touching up high-wear spots later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="room-by-room" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Concrete Floor Paint Is Best for Each Room?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use an epoxy-modified acrylic in the garage, a breathable acrylic in the basement, an acrylic enamel on patios, a pro-grade acrylic in workshops and a slip-resistant coating on steps. Each room puts a different load on the floor, and the right paint follows from that load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is the best paint for a garage floor?</h3>

          <p>
            Choose an epoxy-modified acrylic such as the BEHR or Rust-Oleum one-part paint, etch or grind first, and wait out the full vehicle cure. If you work on cars, set jack stands on plywood pads so they cannot punch through the film. If you want a garage floor that genuinely shrugs off hot tires, go to our ranking of the <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">best garage floor epoxy</a> and plan a weekend project instead of an afternoon.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is the best paint for a basement floor?</h3>

          <p>
            Moisture testing matters more here than anywhere else, because below-grade slabs pass water vapor. Use a breathable acrylic or epoxy-modified paint labeled for basement floors, skip alkyds, and run a dehumidifier while it cures. Our separate guide to the <a href="/best-basement-floor-paint/">best basement floor paint</a> goes deeper into damp-slab products and what to do when the plastic-sheet test fails.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is the best paint for a patio or porch slab?</h3>

          <p>
            Outdoor concrete needs color that holds up to UV, some flexibility for freeze and thaw, and grip when wet. An acrylic enamel such as Benjamin Moore Floor &amp; Patio handles all three well, and an anti-slip additive or a textured coating on the steps is inexpensive insurance. For wood porches and mixed wood-and-concrete entries, see our <a href="/best-porch-paint/">best porch paint</a> picks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What works in a workshop or utility room?</h3>

          <p>
            Boots, carts and dropped tools reward a harder, faster-drying acrylic like Tread-Plex. Consider painting walkway lines or a contrasting border in a safety color so traffic routes and steps stand out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should go on stairs, ramps and pool decks?</h3>

          <p>
            A slip-resistant product such as Sure Step. Smooth gloss paint on a wet step is a genuine fall hazard, and no finish looks good enough to justify that risk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Long Does Painted Concrete Last?
          </h2>

          <p>
            With good prep, one-part floor paint commonly lasts three to five years in a garage, five to seven in a basement or utility room, and two to four on an exposed patio, where UV and freeze-thaw take a toll. Care and timely maintenance coats decide whether a floor reaches the top of that range.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why are the ranges so wide?</h3>

          <p>
            Because the slab, the traffic and the climate count for more than the brand. A floor that was etched, dry and properly cured lands at the top of the range, while a floor painted over a sealer may not make it through its first winter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How should you clean a painted floor?</h3>

          <p>
            Sweep or dust-mop grit regularly, since sand and road grit act like sandpaper underfoot. Mop with plain water or a mild, pH-neutral cleaner, and avoid harsh solvents and strong degreasers that can soften acrylic films. Wipe up oil, brake fluid and gasoline promptly. Put felt pads under furniture legs and a rubber mat where a workbench stool rolls back and forth.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When should you add a maintenance coat?</h3>

          <p>
            Before the floor fails. When the traffic lanes look thin but the paint is still firmly bonded, clean the floor, lightly scuff-sand those areas and roll a single maintenance coat. That costs a gallon or two and an afternoon. Once paint is peeling in sheets, the floor needs stripping or grinding back to bare concrete, which is several times the work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you know a floor has outgrown paint?</h3>

          <p>
            If you find yourself repainting a garage every two years, the space needs more than paint. A two-part system, or a penetrating sealer that leaves the concrete bare but protected, costs more up front and can cost less over a decade. Our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">best concrete sealers</a> covers the bare-concrete route for owners who would rather never repaint.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes With Concrete Floor Paint
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting over a sealer or curing compound</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New slabs are often finished with a curing compound, and older floors have frequently been sealed at some point. Paint grips that layer instead of the concrete and peels away with it. If water beads anywhere on the floor, strip or grind the surface before painting, whatever the can promises about adhesion.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the moisture test on a basement slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water vapor rising through a below-grade slab pushes paint off from underneath, leaving blisters and white, powdery patches. A day under taped plastic sheeting exposes the problem before you buy a single can. If droplets form, fix the source of the moisture first or choose a product labeled for damp conditions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using interior wall paint on the floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wall paint is made to look good on vertical surfaces nobody walks on. Put it on a floor and it stays soft, scuffs under shoes and wears through within weeks. Floor enamels use harder resins built for abrasion and repeated cleaning, so saving twenty dollars a gallon on the wrong paint costs you the whole job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying it on thick to save a coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy coat feels efficient, but it stays soft underneath, holds moisture and is much more likely to peel or wrinkle. Floor paints are designed to build up in two thin coats. Roll until the film looks even and slightly translucent at the edges, then let it dry fully before the second.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Parking on it too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most floor paints can be walked on after a day but need around a week, and longer in cool weather, before a car sits on them. Warm tires soften under-cured paint, grab it and pull it up as they roll away. Park outside until the full vehicle cure on the can has passed.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best paint for a concrete floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the best choice is an epoxy-modified acrylic floor paint such as BEHR Premium 1-Part Epoxy: harder than plain latex, yet it still rolls on like ordinary paint. A premium acrylic enamel looks better on patios and in living spaces, and a slip-resistant coating suits stairs and wet areas. If a vehicle parks on the floor daily, a two-part system serves better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to etch concrete before painting it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most cases, yes. Power-troweled garage slabs and smooth basement floors are too dense for paint to grip, and an etch opens the surface so the paint can bond. Older, rough or previously ground slabs may already have enough profile. Use texture as the guide: if the floor feels smoother than medium sandpaper, etch or grind it before you paint.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I paint concrete that already has paint on it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, provided the old paint is firmly bonded and compatible. Clean it, scuff-sand off the gloss, and check adhesion by pressing and ripping off duct tape in several spots. Wherever old paint comes up with the tape, scrape or grind back to bare concrete. Do not put latex over a glossy oil-based floor enamel without a bonding primer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does concrete floor paint take to dry?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most one-part floor paints feel dry in one to four hours and accept a second coat after about four to twelve hours, depending on the product and the weather. Light foot traffic is usually fine after a day, furniture after a few days and vehicles after roughly a week. Cool, humid conditions slow down every one of those stages.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is concrete floor paint slippery when wet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Smooth satin and gloss floor paints can get slippery when wet, particularly outdoors and on stairs. For steps, ramps, pool decks and entries, mix an anti-slip additive into the final coat or use a textured product such as INSL-X Sure Step or KILZ Over Armor. Indoors, satin is usually safe on dry floors, but put mats by the doors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does painted concrete last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With good prep, plan on roughly three to five years in a garage with car traffic, five to seven years in a basement or utility space and two to four years on an exposed patio. Floors that were thoroughly cleaned, etched and moisture-tested last longest, and rolling a maintenance coat before the paint wears through stretches the life considerably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is paint or epoxy better for a garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two-part epoxy is tougher and thicker, and it resists hot tire pickup, chemicals and abrasion far better. One-part floor paint costs less, goes on more easily and forgives more mistakes, and it is perfectly adequate for garages used for storage, hobbies or occasional parking. If you park every day and want a floor that lasts many years, the two-part coating justifies its extra cost.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

