        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For concrete floors that carry feet rather than tires, a one-part epoxy-modified acrylic such as BEHR Premium gives the best balance of toughness, ease and price, and Rust-Oleum's EpoxyShield paint is a close second for garages. Pick Benjamin Moore Floor &amp; Patio where appearance matters, Tread-Plex where boots and carts do, and Sure Step or Over Armor wherever traction or a rough slab is the real issue.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever can you choose, the result is settled before you open it. Run the water-drop and plastic-sheet checks, degrease and etch, roll two thin coats in mild weather and let the floor cure fully before loading it. Do that, and a painted slab looks good for years and comes back with a single coat when it starts to wear.
          </p>
